/**
 * dbml-sharepoint PROBE: PROJECTED LOOKUP FIELD (DEPENDENT LOOKUP)
 *
 * REVISION: {{ revision }}
 *
 * TWO QUESTIONS. The first is the one this probe was written for; the second
 * was added for issue #184 and shares its fixture.
 *
 * QUESTION ONE, in two halves:
 *
 *   Can a lookup's "additional field" projection be created BY SCRIPT, and
 *   does it auto-populate with the target's real Title?
 *
 * A programme RAID log wants a RelatedRisk lookup whose PICKER shows only live
 * risks (via a calculated display column `LiveRiskTitle` that blanks once
 * Status=Closed), while VIEWS show the real Title. That needs two columns on
 * the source list: the primary lookup (ShowField=LiveRiskTitle) plus a
 * DEPENDENT lookup field that projects Title. Dependent fields are the UI's
 * "add a column to show each of these additional fields", and the linkage is
 * read-only through the normal field object model (IsDependentLookup,
 * PrimaryFieldId, DependentLookupInternalNames). The declarative mechanism is
 * the `FieldRef` attribute on a second Lookup field referencing the primary's
 * GUID. Whether createfieldasxml honours FieldRef in SharePoint Online is the
 * open question. This probe writes.
 *
 * WHAT IT ASKS. Ids follow the grammar in `test/manual/SURFACES.md`:
 * `<surface>.<scope>.<question>`. The old mnemonic each one replaces is
 * given beside it, because the prose below and every run reported against
 * this probe quote the mnemonics.
 *
 *   BOOT
 *        target + source lists, Status choice, LiveTitle calc, two items
 *   field.lookup.control-primary-lookup-created  (PRIM)
 *        primary RelatedRisk lookup created (ShowField=LiveTitle)
 *   field.lookup.dependent-fieldref-accepted     (DEPCR)
 *        dependent field (FieldRef -> primary) accepted by createfieldasxml
 *   field.lookup.isdependentlookup-readback      (DEPFLAG)
 *        the created field reads back IsDependentLookup=true
 *   field.lookup.primary-lists-dependent         (DEPLINK)
 *        the primary lists it in DependentLookupInternalNames
 *   field.lookup.dependent-fill-live-label       (DEPPOP)
 *        setting RelatedRisk to an OPEN risk fills the dependent Title
 *   field.lookup.dependent-fill-blank-label      (DEPLIVE)
 *        for a CLOSED risk (LiveTitle blank) the dependent still shows Title
 *   field.lookup.control-present-target-id-write-accepted
 *        an item write naming a target row that EXISTS is accepted here
 *   field.lookup.control-absent-target-id-names-no-row
 *        the id the write below uses answers 404 from the target list
 *   field.lookup.absent-target-id-write
 *        what an item write answers when the id names no target row
 *   field.lookup.absent-target-id-readback
 *        what an accepted row stores, and what the lookup expands to
 *
 * The probe's own surface is `field` and every question is a question about
 * the lookup column type, so nothing here files elsewhere. `BOOT` keeps its
 * mnemonic: it reports a bootstrap failure rather than a question, and
 * BOOT-prefixed ids are exempt from the grammar by design.
 *
 * RUN 1, 2026-08-28, revision d583e170, sandbox Team Site, through the test
 * agent's autonomous lane, with CLEANUP so DEPCR re-ran the create. Seven
 * questions answered. Two facts fell out:
 *
 *   createfieldasxml honours FieldRef. A fresh dependent field was ACCEPTED
 *   (HTTP 200) and read back IsDependentLookup=true, PrimaryFieldId set,
 *   LookupField=Title, ReadOnlyField=true, and listed in the primary's
 *   DependentLookupInternalNames. The open question is answered yes.
 *
 *   The projected TEXT is not REST-readable. $select=RelatedRiskTitle returns
 *   HTTP 400 "The field or property 'RelatedRiskTitle' does not exist";
 *   $select=RelatedRiskTitleId returns the projected Id; $select=RelatedRisk/
 *   Title is also rejected. So the dependent field's Id auto-populates and is
 *   the only part readable at the item level; the projected Title is
 *   materialised when a view renders it, never on the item. Verification must
 *   therefore check the field schema and the projected Id, never the projected
 *   text. verifyDependentField in _field_reconcile.js.j2 already reads the
 *   schema, which this run confirms is the right (and only) check.
 *
 * QUESTION TWO (issue #184), the cross-web arm:
 *
 *   What does SharePoint Online do with a Lookup whose TARGET LIST IS IN
 *   ANOTHER WEB?
 *
 * `analysis/checks/_naming.py` refuses a `ref` that crosses `site_role` with
 * "SharePoint lookups cannot span webs", and `analysis/limits.py` records how
 * far that is sourced. The short version: Microsoft Support says lookup
 * columns "should be created and used within the same site", which is advice
 * about the column pane rather than a documented refusal by
 * `_api/web/lists/.../fields`; the server object model documents the opposite
 * for itself, with an `SPFieldCollection.AddLookup` overload that takes a
 * target web id; and nothing states what the REST surface this project writes
 * actually does. That is what this arm measures.
 *
 * WHICH TOPOLOGY IT MEASURES, which is narrower than the rule it is parked
 * against. The arm finds its target through `web/webs`, so by default the
 * other web is a CHILD SUBWEB of the pasted site and both ends sit in one
 * site collection. The rule compares two `site_role` labels, and each role is
 * built and deployed with its own `--site-url`, so two roles can be any two
 * sites in the tenant, unrelated site collections included. A result here
 * reads for the pair of webs the run used and no further. A refusal is
 * measured at the closest two webs can be, which is the case for keeping the
 * rule; an acceptance that also resolves a row from over there would disprove
 * the rule for that pair alone, so a relaxation built on it has to be limited
 * to the same topology. An operator who wants a distant pair measured sets
 * OTHER_WEB_URL below to a web in another site collection. Either way every
 * cross-web row carries the pair it was measured at, and the control row
 * compares the two site ids and says which of the two topologies it was.
 *
 * WHAT IT DEPENDS ON, AND WHAT IT OBSERVES, kept apart because the AGENTS.md
 * corollary says a measurement that asserts over its own observations kills
 * itself the moment it starts working. The four controls are the values this
 * arm DEPENDS on; nothing below them asserts an outcome. A control that did
 * not hold voids the rows it carries rather than letting them settle, because
 * a refusal read off a run that cannot create anything is indistinguishable,
 * once it is in the table, from a refusal SharePoint actually gave.
 *
 *   field.cross-web.control-other-web-has-a-list
 *        DEPENDS: a web whose Id reads back and DIFFERS from this page's own
 *        web is reachable, and it holds a readable generic list. Both ids are
 *        required rather than reported, because OTHER_WEB_URL can name this
 *        page's web or redirect to it, and every column below would then be
 *        an ordinary same-web lookup recorded as a cross-web one, which is a
 *        PASS capable of weakening the rule this arm is parked against. Read,
 *        never created: creating a web is a far wider write than anything
 *        else this probe does, and a site with no second web is a site where
 *        the question cannot be asked rather than one where the answer is no.
 *        The row also REPORTS whether that web is in this site collection or
 *        another one, by comparing the two site ids. That comparison is
 *        reported and never required, because the topology is what the rows
 *        below are read against rather than something this arm needs to hold.
 *   field.cross-web.control-other-web-list-has-an-item
 *        DEPENDS: that list holds a readable row whose Title DIFFERS from the
 *        Title on the row of this web's target list carrying the same
 *        list-local id, which is the baseline the resolution rows compare an
 *        expansion with. Nonempty is not enough: a lookup id is list-local,
 *        so a column that quietly repointed expands that local row, and a
 *        baseline equal to it compares equal and reads as a pass. A local
 *        target list with no such row discriminates too, because a repointed
 *        column then expands nothing. Read, never created, for the same
 *        reason as above: this probe writes to no web but its own, and
 *        neither Title is printed.
 *   field.cross-web.control-same-web-lookup-created
 *        DEPENDS: the same createfieldasxml call, with the target list in
 *        THIS web, is accepted BY THIS RUN, and the column it leaves reads
 *        back as a Lookup at that list. Without it a refusal below says the
 *        method did not work here, not that the web boundary refused it. A
 *        column an earlier run left behind is not a control, because a run
 *        whose creates are all failing would read its own leftovers as an
 *        acceptance and file the refusals below as findings.
 *   field.cross-web.control-same-web-webid-accepted
 *        DEPENDS: the same call carrying `WebId="{guid}"`, where that guid is
 *        THIS web, is accepted by this run. It separates the attribute from
 *        the boundary: a createfieldasxml that will not take `WebId` at all
 *        makes the row below a result about the spelling. It isolates a
 *        refusal only. A `WebId` silently ignored for a local target reads
 *        back the same either way, and is caught instead by the readback row,
 *        where an ignored attribute leaves the field pointing at this web.
 *   field.cross-web.createfieldasxml-other-web-refused
 *        OBSERVES: what comes back when `List="{guid}"` names a list in the
 *        other web. The status and the message, whichever way it goes.
 *   field.cross-web.webid-attribute-refused
 *        OBSERVES: the same, with `WebId="{guid}"` naming the other web
 *        explicitly. A separate request, so a separate row: the server
 *        object model's cross-web overload takes a web id, and the two
 *        spellings may not answer alike.
 *   field.cross-web.list-spelling-targets-other-web
 *   field.cross-web.webid-spelling-targets-other-web
 *        OBSERVES: if that spelling was ACCEPTED, does the column it left
 *        read back LookupList and LookupWebId still pointing at the other
 *        web? An accepted create that silently repoints at this web is the
 *        failure class this project exists to catch, and an HTTP 200 alone
 *        cannot tell the two apart.
 *   field.cross-web.list-spelling-resolves-remote-item
 *   field.cross-web.webid-spelling-resolves-remote-item
 *        OBSERVES: does that column carry a real row from the other web? It
 *        is set to an item id read out of that list, whatever the row above
 *        observed about its metadata, and the stored id and the expansion are
 *        read back. Metadata that persisted is the weaker claim of the two: a
 *        field can save the properties asked for, read them back
 *        byte-identical and still resolve nothing, which is the failure class
 *        AGENTS.md names, and a field that reads back odd metadata and still
 *        carries the row is the answer this arm is for. A lookup id is
 *        list-local, so the expansion is compared with the Title read from
 *        the chosen row in the other web: any nonempty title would pass a
 *        column that quietly resolved a row of this web with the same number.
 *        Neither title reaches the transcript, and neither does a length or a
 *        digest of one: an exact length beside an unsalted 32-bit hash of a
 *        short human-written string is recoverable from a transcript, which
 *        is pasted back into a public issue. The comparison is the evidence
 *        the row rests on, and it carries none of the value.
 *
 * ONE ROW PER SPELLING for those four, rather than one verdict over both.
 * The two are separate requests that may answer differently, and a row
 * covering both has to choose one verdict when they do: a column observed
 * repointing at this web disproves the claim whether or not the other column
 * could be read, and a combined row reporting that as unestablished discards
 * the observation that settled it. A spelling whose create was taken and
 * whose column then did not read back is voided rather than set to a value,
 * because writing a lookup property no column carries is refused for the name
 * and says nothing about the web boundary.
 *
 * These file under a `cross-web` scope of their own rather than under
 * `lookup`, for the reason `test/manual/SURFACES.md` gives: every result
 * recorded under `lookup` was measured with both ends in one web, and a
 * result about a column inside a web does not transfer to the boundary
 * between two.
 *
 * QUESTION THREE (issue #31), the absent-id arm:
 *
 *   Does SharePoint check at write time that a lookup id names a row in the
 *   target list?
 *
 * `website/docs/concepts/relationships.md` tells adopters that a DBML `Ref` is
 * a pointer and not a foreign key. Microsoft Learn was searched for the write
 * side on 2026-09-14 and holds only the neighbouring question: the `Field`
 * element documents `RelationshipDeleteBehavior` and its `None` default, and no
 * Microsoft page states what an item write does with a lookup id no row
 * carries. So the page marks write-time enforcement not established, and
 * `analysis/limits.py` parks it on the rows below.
 *
 * WHAT IT DEPENDS ON, AND WHAT IT OBSERVES, kept apart for the reason the
 * cross-web arm gives above.
 *
 *   field.lookup.control-present-target-id-write-accepted
 *        DEPENDS: an item create carrying a RelatedRiskId that a target row
 *        DOES carry is accepted BY THIS RUN. Without it a refusal below says
 *        this site refuses item creates, not that the absent id was refused.
 *   field.lookup.control-absent-target-id-names-no-row
 *        DEPENDS: the id the write below uses answers HTTP 404 from the target
 *        list. It is the highest id that list holds plus a margin, READ rather
 *        than assumed, because against a site that already holds that row the
 *        write below is an ordinary one and nothing would say so.
 *   field.lookup.absent-target-id-write
 *        OBSERVES: the status, and the message if there is one, of an item
 *        create whose RelatedRiskId names that absent id. Whichever way it
 *        goes: an acceptance and a refusal are both answers here.
 *   field.lookup.absent-target-id-readback
 *        OBSERVES: for an accepted write, what the stored id reads back and
 *        what `$expand` gives for the lookup. A write SharePoint accepted and
 *        then emptied is a different fact from one that keeps the id, and a
 *        report reading the second cannot tell it from a live reference.
 *
 * ALL FOUR VOID TOGETHER, controls included, when RelatedRisk does not read
 * back pointing at this probe's own target list. CLEANUP ships false, so a
 * column an earlier run left is the ordinary case, and every row above reads
 * the target list: an id absent from a list the lookup does not consult
 * bounds nothing, and the present-id control passes on any number the
 * lookup's real target happens to hold.
 *
 * Both ends are generic lists in one web, so these file under `lookup`.
 *
 * NOT YET RUN. Nothing below has been measured against a live site.
 */

(async () => {
  // ---- Operator gate -------------------------------------------------
  const CONFIRMED = false;
  const ALLOW_WRITES = false;
  const CLEANUP = false;
  // Empty means the arm finds its own target through web/webs, which is a
  // child subweb of this site; naming a web in another site collection
  // measures that pair instead. The arm reads the other web and never writes.
  // A URL that resolves to THIS web voids the arm rather than measuring it.
  const OTHER_WEB_URL = '';

  const pageCtx = window._spPageContextInfo;
  if (!pageCtx) {
    console.error('[FATAL] No _spPageContextInfo. Paste this into a SharePoint page.');
    return;
  }
  const WEB = pageCtx.webAbsoluteUrl;

  const log = (level, msg) => console.log(`[${level}] ${msg}`);

  log('INFO', 'probe revision {{ revision }}. Quote this when reporting results.');

  const getDigest = async () => {
    const res = await fetch(`${WEB}/_api/contextinfo`, {
      method: 'POST', headers: { Accept: 'application/json;odata=verbose' },
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`contextinfo failed: HTTP ${res.status}`);
    const body = await res.json();
    return body.d.GetContextWebInformation.FormDigestValue;
  };

  const spGet = async (path) => {
    const res = await fetch(`${WEB}/_api/${path}`, {
      headers: { Accept: 'application/json;odata=nometadata' },
    });
    return { ok: res.ok, status: res.status, body: await res.json().catch(() => null) };
  };

  // A read rooted at ANOTHER web's API. The cross-web arm needs the other
  // web's own endpoint, because `${WEB}/_api` only ever answers for this one.
  const spGetAt = async (webUrl, path) => {
    const res = await fetch(`${webUrl}/_api/${path}`, {
      headers: { Accept: 'application/json;odata=nometadata' },
    });
    return { ok: res.ok, status: res.status, body: await res.json().catch(() => null) };
  };

  const readFailed = (r) => !r.ok || r.body === null;

  // Hoisted: the absent-id arm compares a target list before the cross-web arm does.
  const asGuid = (value) => String(value === undefined || value === null ? '' : value)
    .replace(/[{}]/g, '').toLowerCase();

  const spPost = async (path, payload, digest, extraHeaders = {}) => {
    const res = await fetch(`${WEB}/_api/${path}`, {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {
        Accept: 'application/json;odata=nometadata',
        'Content-Type': 'application/json;odata=nometadata',
        'X-RequestDigest': digest,
        ...extraHeaders,
      },
      body: JSON.stringify(payload),
    });
    const text = await res.text();
    let parsed = null;
    try { parsed = JSON.parse(text); } catch { /* plain text */ }
    return { ok: res.ok, status: res.status, body: parsed, text };
  };

  const resetList = async (title) => {
    if (!CLEANUP) return false;
    if (!ALLOW_WRITES) return false;
    const found = await spGet(`web/lists/getbytitle('${title}')`);
    if (!found.ok) return false;
    let digest = await getDigest();
    const gone = await spPost(`web/lists/getbytitle('${title}')/recycle`, {}, digest);
    if (gone.ok) log('OK', `CLEANUP: recycled '${title}'.`);
    else log('FAIL', `CLEANUP: could not recycle '${title}': HTTP ${gone.status}`);
    return gone.ok;
  };

  // STATE is the five-value vocabulary in test/manual/SURFACES.md, carried
  // beside the prose. An explicit state passed to record() wins over the
  // classifier, which is the default for rows nobody has ruled on yet.
  // ABORTED is open: it means the fixture never built, so the question was
  // never asked and the run has nothing to settle it with.
  const OPEN_HEADS = ['NOT ESTABLISHED', 'SHORT', 'ABORTED'];
  const AWAITING_CAPTURE_HEADS = ['MANUAL', 'NOT REACHED'];
  const stateFor = (outcome) => {
    if (AWAITING_CAPTURE_HEADS.some((p) => outcome.startsWith(p))) return 'awaiting-capture';
    if (OPEN_HEADS.some((p) => outcome.startsWith(p))) return 'open';
    return 'settled';
  };
  const RESULTS = [];
  const expect = (id, question) => {
    RESULTS.push({
      id, question, outcome: 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
      evidence: 'the run did not reach this question', state: 'open',
    });
  };
  const record = (id, question, outcome, evidence, state) => {
    const next = { question, outcome, evidence, state: state || stateFor(outcome) };
    const row = RESULTS.find((r) => r.id === id);
    if (row) Object.assign(row, next);
    else RESULTS.push({ id, ...next });
    const level = outcome === 'PASS' ? 'OK' : outcome === 'FAIL' ? 'FAIL' : 'INFO';
    log(level, `${id}: ${outcome}. ${question}`);
    if (evidence) console.log(`      evidence: ${evidence}`);
  };
  const report = () => {
    console.log('\n==================== RESULTS ====================');
    for (const r of RESULTS) {
      console.log(`${r.id.padEnd(8)} ${r.state.padEnd(16)} ${r.outcome.padEnd(16)} ${r.question}`);
      if (r.evidence) console.log(`       ${r.evidence}`);
    }
    console.log('=================================================');
    const open = RESULTS.filter((r) => r.state !== 'settled').length;
    console.log(`${RESULTS.length} question(s); ${RESULTS.length - open} answered, ${open} open.`);
    if (open) console.log('A question with no observation is NOT a pass. Report it as open.');
    console.log('Copy this whole block back verbatim.');
  };
  // A row whose step never answered is voided by id, because a verdict read
  // off a failed step is indistinguishable downstream from a measured one.
  const voidAll = (ids, reason) => {
    for (const id of ids) {
      record(id, RESULTS.find((r) => r.id === id).question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED', reason, 'void');
    }
  };

  const TARGET = 'dbmlsp Probe ProjTarget';
  const SOURCE = 'dbmlsp Probe ProjSource';
  const targetFields = `web/lists/getbytitle('${TARGET}')/fields`;
  const sourceFields = `web/lists/getbytitle('${SOURCE}')/fields`;
  const sourceItems = `web/lists/getbytitle('${SOURCE}')/items`;
  const targetItems = `web/lists/getbytitle('${TARGET}')/items`;

  if (!CONFIRMED) {
    log('INFO', `Would create '${TARGET}' (a Status choice, a LiveTitle`);
    log('INFO', 'calculated column and two risk rows) and ' + `'${SOURCE}' (a`);
    log('INFO', 'RelatedRisk lookup into the target, plus a FieldRef-linked');
    log('INFO', 'dependent lookup projecting Title), then set RelatedRisk on');
    log('INFO', 'source rows and read back whether the dependent auto-fills.');
    log('INFO', 'It would add two more source rows for the absent-id arm: one');
    log('INFO', 'naming a target row that exists, as the control, and one');
    log('INFO', 'naming an id it first read back as absent from the target.');
    log('INFO', 'It would then add four more lookup columns to the source');
    log('INFO', 'list for the cross-web arm: two at a list in THIS web as the');
    log('INFO', 'controls, and two at a list in the other web, which it READS');
    log('INFO', 'and never creates (a subweb of this site, or OTHER_WEB_URL if');
    log('INFO', 'that is set). For each cross-web column that is accepted it sets');
    log('INFO', 'a source row to an item over there. It writes to no web but this one.');
    log('INFO', 'Nothing has been written. Set CONFIRMED and ALLOW_WRITES to true.');
    return;
  }
  if (!ALLOW_WRITES) {
    log('INFO', 'CONFIRMED, but ALLOW_WRITES is false and this probe must write.');
    log('INFO', 'Set ALLOW_WRITES = true to proceed. Stopping.');
    return;
  }

  expect('BOOT', 'target + source lists, Status choice, LiveTitle calc and two target rows exist');
  expect('field.lookup.fixture-lists-created', 'the target and source lists read back as generic lists (BaseTemplate 100)');
  expect('field.lookup.control-primary-lookup-created', 'primary RelatedRisk lookup created (ShowField=LiveTitle)');
  expect('field.lookup.dependent-fieldref-accepted', 'dependent field with FieldRef -> primary accepted by createfieldasxml');
  expect('field.lookup.isdependentlookup-readback', 'the created field reads back IsDependentLookup=true');
  expect('field.lookup.primary-lists-dependent', 'the primary lists the dependent in DependentLookupInternalNames');
  expect('field.lookup.dependent-fill-live-label', 'setting RelatedRisk to an OPEN risk fills the dependent Title');
  expect('field.lookup.dependent-fill-blank-label', 'for a CLOSED risk (LiveTitle blank) the dependent still shows the real Title');
  expect('field.lookup.control-present-target-id-write-accepted', 'an item create naming a target row that EXISTS is accepted by this run');
  expect('field.lookup.control-absent-target-id-names-no-row', 'the id the write below uses names no row in the target list');
  expect('field.lookup.absent-target-id-write', 'what an item create answers when RelatedRiskId names an id no target row carries');
  expect('field.lookup.absent-target-id-readback', 'what an accepted absent-id row stores, and what the lookup expands to');
  expect('field.cross-web.control-other-web-has-a-list', 'a web whose Id reads back different from the web this page is on holds a readable generic list to point a lookup at');
  expect('field.cross-web.control-other-web-list-has-an-item', 'that list in the other web holds a readable row whose Title differs from the row of this web sharing its list-local id');
  expect('field.cross-web.control-same-web-lookup-created', 'the same createfieldasxml call is accepted when the target list is in THIS web');
  expect('field.cross-web.control-same-web-webid-accepted', 'the same call carrying a WebId is accepted when that WebId is THIS web');
  expect('field.cross-web.createfieldasxml-other-web-refused', 'what createfieldasxml answers when List names a list in the other web');
  expect('field.cross-web.webid-attribute-refused', 'what it answers when WebId names the other web explicitly');
  expect('field.cross-web.list-spelling-targets-other-web', 'a field accepted through the List spelling reads back LookupList and LookupWebId still pointing at the other web');
  expect('field.cross-web.webid-spelling-targets-other-web', 'a field accepted through the List+WebId spelling reads back LookupList and LookupWebId still pointing at the other web');
  expect('field.cross-web.list-spelling-resolves-remote-item', 'a field accepted through the List spelling stores a real item id from the other web and expands to the Title on that row');
  expect('field.cross-web.webid-spelling-resolves-remote-item', 'a field accepted through the List+WebId spelling stores a real item id from the other web and expands to the Title on that row');

  await resetList(SOURCE);
  await resetList(TARGET);
  let digest = await getDigest();

  // ---- Bootstrap: lists ----------------------------------------------
  const ensureList = async (title) => {
    const existing = await spGet(`web/lists/getbytitle('${title}')`);
    if (existing.ok) return { id: existing.body.Id, made: false };
    digest = await getDigest();
    const made = await spPost('web/lists', {
      Title: title, BaseTemplate: 100, Description: 'dbml-sharepoint probe list. Safe to delete.',
    }, digest);
    return { id: made.ok ? made.body.Id : null, made: made.ok, text: made.text };
  };

  const target = await ensureList(TARGET);
  const source = await ensureList(SOURCE);
  if (!target.id || !source.id) {
    record('BOOT', 'create the two probe lists', 'FAIL',
           `target=${target.id || `HTTP ${target.text.slice(0, 160)}`} source=${source.id || `HTTP ${source.text.slice(0, 160)}`}`);
    return report();
  }
  // Read back on reuse as well as on create, because a list found by title may be a library.
  const LISTS_ROW = 'field.lookup.fixture-lists-created';
  const kinds = [];
  let generic = true;
  for (const title of [TARGET, SOURCE]) {
    const read = await spGet(`web/lists/getbytitle('${title}')?$select=BaseTemplate`);
    const served = !readFailed(read) && typeof read.body === 'object' && read.body.BaseTemplate !== undefined;
    kinds.push(served ? `'${title}' BaseTemplate=${JSON.stringify(read.body.BaseTemplate)}`
      : `'${title}' served no BaseTemplate (HTTP ${read.status})`);
    generic = generic && served && read.body.BaseTemplate === 100;
  }
  record(LISTS_ROW, RESULTS.find((r) => r.id === LISTS_ROW).question, generic ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL',
         `${kinds.join('; ')}, declared 100`);
  if (!generic) {
    voidAll(RESULTS.map((r) => r.id).filter((id) => id !== LISTS_ROW && id !== 'BOOT'),
            `the fixture ${LISTS_ROW} did not hold: ${kinds.join('; ')}`);
    return report();
  }

  const addField = async (fieldsPath, schemaXml) => {
    digest = await getDigest();
    return spPost(`${fieldsPath}/createfieldasxml`, {
      parameters: { SchemaXml: schemaXml, Options: 8 },
    }, digest);
  };
  const fieldExists = async (fieldsPath, name) =>
    (await spGet(`${fieldsPath}/getbyinternalnameortitle('${name}')`)).ok;

  // ---- Target: Status choice + LiveTitle calculated ------------------
  if (!(await fieldExists(targetFields, 'Status'))) {
    const made = await addField(targetFields,
      `<Field Type="Choice" DisplayName="Status" Name="Status" Format="Dropdown">`
      + `<CHOICES><CHOICE>Open</CHOICE><CHOICE>Closed</CHOICE></CHOICES>`
      + `<Default>Open</Default></Field>`);
    if (!made.ok) {
      record('BOOT', 'create the Status choice column', 'FAIL',
             `HTTP ${made.status}: ${made.text.slice(0, 300)}`);
      return report();
    }
  }
  if (!(await fieldExists(targetFields, 'LiveTitle'))) {
    const made = await addField(targetFields,
      `<Field Type="Calculated" DisplayName="LiveTitle" Name="LiveTitle" ResultType="Text">`
      + `<Formula>=IF(Status="Closed","",Title)</Formula>`
      + `<FieldRefs><FieldRef Name="Status"/><FieldRef Name="Title"/></FieldRefs></Field>`);
    if (!made.ok) {
      record('BOOT', 'create the LiveTitle calculated column', 'FAIL',
             `HTTP ${made.status}: ${made.text.slice(0, 300)}`);
      return report();
    }
  }

  // ---- Target rows: one open, one closed -----------------------------
  let openId = null; let closedId = null;
  const existingRows = await spGet(`${targetItems}?$select=Id,Title&$top=10`);
  const rows = (existingRows.ok && existingRows.body && existingRows.body.value) || [];
  for (const row of rows) {
    if (row.Title === 'Risk Open') openId = row.Id;
    if (row.Title === 'Risk Closed') closedId = row.Id;
  }
  const makeTargetRow = async (title, status) => {
    digest = await getDigest();
    const res = await spPost(targetItems, { Title: title, Status: status }, digest);
    return res.ok ? res.body.Id : null;
  };
  if (openId === null) openId = await makeTargetRow('Risk Open', 'Open');
  if (closedId === null) closedId = await makeTargetRow('Risk Closed', 'Closed');
  if (openId === null || closedId === null) {
    record('BOOT', 'create the open and closed target rows', 'FAIL',
           `open=${openId} closed=${closedId}`);
    return report();
  }

  // ---- Primary RelatedRisk lookup ------------------------------------
  if (!(await fieldExists(sourceFields, 'RelatedRisk'))) {
    const made = await addField(sourceFields,
      `<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="Related Risk" Name="RelatedRisk"`
      + ` List="{${target.id}}" ShowField="LiveTitle"/>`);
    if (!made.ok) {
      record('field.lookup.control-primary-lookup-created', 'create the primary RelatedRisk lookup', 'FAIL',
             `HTTP ${made.status}: ${made.text.slice(0, 300)}`);
      return report();
    }
  }
  const primaryField = await spGet(`${sourceFields}/getbyinternalnameortitle('RelatedRisk')`);
  if (readFailed(primaryField)) {
    record('field.lookup.control-primary-lookup-created', 'read back the primary RelatedRisk field', 'FAIL',
           `HTTP ${primaryField.status}`);
    return report();
  }
  const primaryId = primaryField.body.Id;
  record('field.lookup.control-primary-lookup-created', 'primary RelatedRisk lookup created (ShowField=LiveTitle)', 'PASS',
         `field Id ${primaryId}`);

  // ---- Dependent field via FieldRef ----------------------------------
  //
  // The acceptance row is about a REQUEST, so it is recorded from the request
  // this run sent. CLEANUP ships false, which makes finding the column already
  // there the ordinary second-run path, and that run asked createfieldasxml
  // nothing.
  const DEP_CREATE_QUESTION = 'dependent field with FieldRef -> primary accepted by createfieldasxml';
  const depExisted = await fieldExists(sourceFields, 'RelatedRiskTitle');
  let depMade = null;
  if (!depExisted) {
    depMade = await addField(sourceFields,
      `<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="Related Risk Title" Name="RelatedRiskTitle"`
      + ` List="{${target.id}}" ShowField="Title" FieldRef="{${primaryId}}" ReadOnly="TRUE"/>`);
  }

  const depField = await spGet(`${sourceFields}/getbyinternalnameortitle('RelatedRiskTitle')`);
  if (depExisted) {
    record('field.lookup.dependent-fieldref-accepted', DEP_CREATE_QUESTION, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           'the field already exists from an earlier run, so this run sent no create. '
           + 'Set CLEANUP = true for a clean answer');
  } else if (!depMade.ok) {
    record('field.lookup.dependent-fieldref-accepted', DEP_CREATE_QUESTION, 'REFUSED',
           `HTTP ${depMade.status}: ${depMade.text.slice(0, 300)}`);
  } else if (readFailed(depField)) {
    record('field.lookup.dependent-fieldref-accepted', DEP_CREATE_QUESTION, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           `HTTP ${depMade.status} on createfieldasxml, but the field did not read back `
           + `(HTTP ${depField.status}). An accepted create that left no column is not an acceptance`);
  } else {
    record('field.lookup.dependent-fieldref-accepted', DEP_CREATE_QUESTION, 'ACCEPTED',
           `created; HTTP ${depMade.status}`);
  }

  if (readFailed(depField)) {
    record('field.lookup.isdependentlookup-readback', 'the created field reads back IsDependentLookup=true', 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           `could not read the field: HTTP ${depField.status}`);
  } else {
    const depLookup = depField.body.IsDependentLookup;
    record('field.lookup.isdependentlookup-readback', 'the created field reads back IsDependentLookup=true',
           depLookup === true ? 'PASS' : (depLookup === false ? 'FAIL' : 'NOT ESTABLISHED'),
           `IsDependentLookup=${depLookup}; PrimaryFieldId=${depField.body.PrimaryFieldId || '(absent)'}; `
           + `TypeAsString=${depField.body.TypeAsString}`);
  }

  // An absent property and an empty one are different answers: the first says
  // this run had nothing to read the linkage from, the second says SharePoint
  // read the linkage back and it is empty.
  const LINK_QUESTION = 'the primary lists the dependent in DependentLookupInternalNames';
  const primaryAfter = await spGet(`${sourceFields}/getbyinternalnameortitle('RelatedRisk')`);
  const primNames = readFailed(primaryAfter) ? undefined : primaryAfter.body.DependentLookupInternalNames;
  if (readFailed(primaryAfter)) {
    record('field.lookup.primary-lists-dependent', LINK_QUESTION, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           `the primary did not read back: HTTP ${primaryAfter.status}`);
  } else if (primNames === undefined || primNames === null) {
    record('field.lookup.primary-lists-dependent', LINK_QUESTION, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           'the primary read back with no DependentLookupInternalNames property, so this '
           + 'run has nothing to read the linkage from');
  } else {
    record('field.lookup.primary-lists-dependent', LINK_QUESTION,
           primNames.length ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL',
           `DependentLookupInternalNames=${JSON.stringify(primNames)}`);
  }

  // ---- Auto-population: open then closed -----------------------------
  const setAndRead = async (riskId) => {
    digest = await getDigest();
    const made = await spPost(sourceItems, { Title: `linked-${riskId}`, RelatedRiskId: riskId }, digest);
    if (!made.ok) return { ok: false, detail: `create HTTP ${made.status}: ${made.text.slice(0, 240)}` };
    const itemId = made.body.Id;
    const def = await spGet(`${sourceItems}(${itemId})`);
    const byId = await spGet(`${sourceItems}(${itemId})?$select=RelatedRiskTitleId`);
    const byText = await spGet(`${sourceItems}(${itemId})?$select=RelatedRiskTitle`);
    return {
      ok: true, itemId, idRead: byId.ok, idStatus: byId.status,
      primaryId: def.ok ? def.body.RelatedRiskId : undefined,
      depId: byId.ok ? byId.body.RelatedRiskTitleId : `HTTP ${byId.status}`,
      depText: byText.ok ? byText.body.RelatedRiskTitle : `HTTP ${byText.status}`,
      detail: def.ok ? '' : `default read HTTP ${def.status}`,
    };
  };

  // Both rows ask what the dependent field fills with. A row that was never
  // created and a projected id that never read back are failed steps, and
  // neither is a projection that came back empty.
  const fillEvidence = (res, riskId) =>
    `primaryId=${res.primaryId} dependentId=${res.depId} (expect ${riskId}) `
    + `dependentText=${res.depText} ${res.detail || ''}`;
  const recordFill = (id, question, res, riskId) => {
    if (!res.ok) {
      record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `the source row was never created, so nothing was set: ${res.detail}`, 'void');
    } else if (!res.idRead) {
      record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `the projected id did not read back (HTTP ${res.idStatus}). `
             + fillEvidence(res, riskId), 'void');
    } else {
      record(id, question, res.depId === riskId ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL', fillEvidence(res, riskId));
    }
  };

  const FILL_ROWS = ['field.lookup.dependent-fill-live-label',
                     'field.lookup.dependent-fill-blank-label'];
  if (readFailed(depField)) {
    voidAll(FILL_ROWS, `the dependent field did not read back (HTTP ${depField.status}), so an `
      + 'empty projection here would say nothing about auto-fill');
  } else {
    recordFill('field.lookup.dependent-fill-live-label',
               'setting RelatedRisk to an OPEN risk auto-fills the dependent (Id propagates)',
               await setAndRead(openId), openId);
    recordFill('field.lookup.dependent-fill-blank-label',
               'for a CLOSED risk (LiveTitle blank) the dependent still carries the real Title',
               await setAndRead(closedId), closedId);
  }

  // ---- Absent-id arm (issue #31) -------------------------------------
  const PRESENT_WRITE_QUESTION = 'an item create naming a target row that EXISTS is accepted by this run';
  const ABSENT_ID_QUESTION = 'the id the write below uses names no row in the target list';
  const ABSENT_WRITE_QUESTION = 'what an item create answers when RelatedRiskId names an id no target row carries';
  const ABSENT_READBACK_QUESTION = 'what an accepted absent-id row stores, and what the lookup expands to';

  const writeRelated = async (label, riskId) => {
    digest = await getDigest();
    return spPost(sourceItems, { Title: label, RelatedRiskId: riskId }, digest);
  };

  const ARM_ROWS = ['field.lookup.control-present-target-id-write-accepted',
                    'field.lookup.control-absent-target-id-names-no-row',
                    'field.lookup.absent-target-id-write',
                    'field.lookup.absent-target-id-readback'];

  // CLEANUP ships false, so a reused RelatedRisk is the ordinary case, and an
  // id absent from THIS target list bounds nothing if the lookup points elsewhere.
  const primaryTarget = asGuid(primaryField.body.LookupList);
  if (primaryTarget !== asGuid(target.id)) {
    voidAll(ARM_ROWS,
            `RelatedRisk reads back LookupList=${primaryTarget || '(absent)'} and this arm reads `
            + `list ${asGuid(target.id)}, so neither the present id nor the absent one is known `
            + 'to be about the list this lookup targets. Set CLEANUP = true to rebuild it');
  } else {
    const present = await writeRelated(`present-${openId}`, openId);
    record('field.lookup.control-present-target-id-write-accepted', PRESENT_WRITE_QUESTION,
           present.ok ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL',
           `RelatedRiskId=${openId} answered HTTP ${present.status}`);

    // The highest id plus a margin, read rather than assumed: on a site already
    // holding that row the write below would be an ordinary one.
    const highest = await spGet(`${targetItems}?$select=Id&$orderby=Id desc&$top=1`);
    const highestRows = (highest.ok && highest.body && highest.body.value) || [];
    const absentId = highestRows.length ? highestRows[0].Id + 1000 : null;
    const absentRead = absentId === null
      ? null : await spGet(`${targetItems}(${absentId})?$select=Id`);
    if (absentId === null) {
      record('field.lookup.control-absent-target-id-names-no-row', ABSENT_ID_QUESTION,
             'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `the target list's ids did not read back (HTTP ${highest.status}), so this run had `
             + 'no id it knows to be absent');
    } else if (absentRead.status === 404) {
      record('field.lookup.control-absent-target-id-names-no-row', ABSENT_ID_QUESTION, 'PASS',
             `item ${absentId} answers HTTP 404 from the list RelatedRisk points at`);
    } else if (absentRead.ok) {
      record('field.lookup.control-absent-target-id-names-no-row', ABSENT_ID_QUESTION, 'FAIL',
             `item ${absentId} reads back, so the target list holds the row this arm needs `
             + 'to be missing');
    } else {
      // Neither 404 nor a row: the read did not answer, which is not the same
      // as an id that is present.
      record('field.lookup.control-absent-target-id-names-no-row', ABSENT_ID_QUESTION,
             'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `reading item ${absentId} answered HTTP ${absentRead.status}, so this run cannot `
             + 'say whether the row is there');
    }

    const ABSENT_ROWS = ['field.lookup.absent-target-id-write',
                         'field.lookup.absent-target-id-readback'];
    if (!present.ok) {
      voidAll(ABSENT_ROWS,
              `an item create naming a target row that exists answered HTTP ${present.status}, so `
              + 'a refusal below would be about creating items here');
    } else if (absentId === null || absentRead.status !== 404) {
      voidAll(ABSENT_ROWS, 'no id was established as absent, so the write below would not be the '
        + 'write this arm asks about');
    } else {
      const absentWrite = await writeRelated(`absent-${absentId}`, absentId);
      record('field.lookup.absent-target-id-write', ABSENT_WRITE_QUESTION,
             absentWrite.ok ? 'ACCEPTED' : 'REFUSED',
             `RelatedRiskId=${absentId} answered HTTP ${absentWrite.status}`
             + (absentWrite.ok ? '' : `: ${absentWrite.text.slice(0, 240)}`));
      if (!absentWrite.ok) {
        record('field.lookup.absent-target-id-readback', ABSENT_READBACK_QUESTION,
               'NOT APPLICABLE', 'the write was refused, so no row carries the absent id');
      } else {
        const itemId = absentWrite.body.Id;
        const stored = await spGet(`${sourceItems}(${itemId})?$select=RelatedRiskId`);
        const expanded = await spGet(
          `${sourceItems}(${itemId})?$select=RelatedRisk/Title&$expand=RelatedRisk`);
        if (readFailed(stored) || readFailed(expanded)) {
          record('field.lookup.absent-target-id-readback', ABSENT_READBACK_QUESTION,
                 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
                 `the accepted row did not read back (stored HTTP ${stored.status}, expansion `
                 + `HTTP ${expanded.status})`, 'void');
        } else {
          const seen = expanded.body.RelatedRisk;
          record('field.lookup.absent-target-id-readback', ABSENT_READBACK_QUESTION, 'OBSERVED',
                 `stored id reads back ${JSON.stringify(stored.body.RelatedRiskId)} for a write of `
                 + `${absentId}; $expand gives `
                 + `${seen === null || seen === undefined ? 'null' : JSON.stringify(seen)}`);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // ---- Cross-web arm (issue #184) ------------------------------------
  //
  // The other web is only ever read, so a refusal below is about the lookup
  // and not about permission on the far side.
  //
  // Evidence carries GUIDs and no web title or URL, because a transcript is
  // pasted back into an issue and a site name in one is a leak.
  const OTHER_WEB_QUESTION = 'a web whose Id reads back different from the web this page is on holds a readable generic list to point a lookup at';
  const OTHER_ITEM_QUESTION = 'that list in the other web holds a readable row whose Title differs from the row of this web sharing its list-local id';
  const SAME_WEB_QUESTION = 'the same createfieldasxml call is accepted when the target list is in THIS web';
  const SAME_WEB_WEBID_QUESTION = 'the same call carrying a WebId is accepted when that WebId is THIS web';
  const BY_LIST_QUESTION = 'what createfieldasxml answers when List names a list in the other web';
  const BY_WEBID_QUESTION = 'what it answers when WebId names the other web explicitly';
  const LIST_READBACK_QUESTION = 'a field accepted through the List spelling reads back LookupList and LookupWebId still pointing at the other web';
  const WEBID_READBACK_QUESTION = 'a field accepted through the List+WebId spelling reads back LookupList and LookupWebId still pointing at the other web';
  const LIST_RESOLVE_QUESTION = 'a field accepted through the List spelling stores a real item id from the other web and expands to the Title on that row';
  const WEBID_RESOLVE_QUESTION = 'a field accepted through the List+WebId spelling stores a real item id from the other web and expands to the Title on that row';

  // A Title is compared and never described: an exact length beside an
  // unsalted 32-bit digest of somebody's production row is recoverable from a
  // transcript, and the comparison is the evidence these rows rest on anyway.
  const titleKind = (value) => (typeof value === 'string'
    ? 'a Title' : `no Title (${value === null ? 'null' : typeof value})`);

  // Uniform shape whichever way it goes, and `asked: false` for a field an
  // earlier run already created: that is not an observation of this run.
  const attemptLookup = async (name, schemaXml) => {
    if (await fieldExists(sourceFields, name)) {
      return { asked: false, detail: `'${name}' already exists from an earlier run` };
    }
    const made = await addField(sourceFields, schemaXml);
    return {
      asked: true,
      ok: made.ok,
      detail: made.ok
        ? `created; HTTP ${made.status}`
        : `HTTP ${made.status}: ${made.text.slice(0, 300)}`,
    };
  };

  // A control states that createfieldasxml takes a same-web lookup on this
  // site, in THIS run, and leaves the column that was asked for. A column an
  // earlier run left behind cannot stand in for that: CLEANUP ships false, so
  // a run whose creates are all failing (a permission change, throttling, a
  // fixture somebody edited) would otherwise read its own leftovers as an
  // acceptance and file the cross-web refusals below as findings.
  const runControl = async (id, question, name, schemaXml) => {
    const made = await attemptLookup(name, schemaXml);
    if (!made.asked) {
      record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `${made.detail}, so this run sent no create and cannot show one would be `
             + 'accepted now. Set CLEANUP = true for a clean answer');
      return false;
    }
    if (!made.ok) {
      record(id, question, 'REFUSED', made.detail);
      return false;
    }
    const back = await spGet(`${sourceFields}/getbyinternalnameortitle('${name}')`
      + `?$select=LookupList,LookupWebId,TypeAsString`);
    if (readFailed(back)) {
      record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `${made.detail}, but the column did not read back (HTTP ${back.status}). `
             + 'An accepted create that left no column is not an acceptance');
      return false;
    }
    const local = back.body.TypeAsString === 'Lookup'
      && asGuid(back.body.LookupList) === asGuid(target.id);
    // LookupWebId is reported and never required: what a control depends on is
    // a create this run sent being taken, and where SharePoint files a local
    // web id is one of the things this arm exists to observe.
    record(id, question, local ? 'ACCEPTED' : 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           `${made.detail}; reads back TypeAsString=${back.body.TypeAsString} `
           + `LookupList=${asGuid(back.body.LookupList)} (expect ${asGuid(target.id)}) `
           + `LookupWebId=${asGuid(back.body.LookupWebId)}`);
    return local;
  };

  const thisWeb = await spGet('web?$select=Id');
  const thisWebId = readFailed(thisWeb) ? null : thisWeb.body.Id;

  // A supplied web is the only candidate, so an operator measuring a distant
  // pair is not handed the nearest subweb instead.
  const suppliedWeb = OTHER_WEB_URL ? await spGetAt(OTHER_WEB_URL, 'web?$select=Id') : null;
  const webs = suppliedWeb || await spGet('web/webs?$select=Id,Url');
  const webRows = suppliedWeb
    ? (readFailed(suppliedWeb) ? [] : [{ Id: suppliedWeb.body.Id, Url: OTHER_WEB_URL }])
    : ((webs.ok && webs.body && webs.body.value) || []);
  let otherWeb = null;
  let otherList = null;
  let otherItemId = null;
  let otherItemTitle;
  let otherItemBaseline = '';
  let otherItemWhy = '';
  let noWebWhy = 'no candidate web held a readable generic list';

  // A candidate is another web only when both ids read back and differ.
  // OTHER_WEB_URL can name the web this page is on, or redirect to it, and
  // every column below would then be an ordinary same-web lookup recorded as
  // a cross-web one, which is a PASS that could be read as weakening the rule.
  const notAnotherWeb = (candidateId) => {
    if (thisWebId === null || thisWebId === undefined) {
      return `this web's Id did not read back (HTTP ${thisWeb.status}), so no candidate can be `
        + 'shown to be a different web';
    }
    if (asGuid(candidateId) === '') {
      return 'the candidate web answered with no Id, so it cannot be shown to be a different web';
    }
    if (asGuid(candidateId) === asGuid(thisWebId)) {
      return `the candidate web is THIS web (${asGuid(thisWebId)}), so a lookup at a list in `
        + 'it would be an ordinary same-web lookup';
    }
    return null;
  };

  // A repointed column expands the row of THIS web carrying the same
  // list-local id, so only a remote Title that differs from that row's can
  // tell a column that resolved from one that quietly repointed.
  const localRows = new Map();
  const localRowAt = async (itemId) => {
    if (!localRows.has(itemId)) {
      const got = await spGet(`${targetItems}?$select=Id,Title&$filter=Id eq ${itemId}`);
      localRows.set(itemId, readFailed(got)
        ? { readable: false, status: got.status }
        : { readable: true, row: ((got.body && got.body.value) || [])[0] });
    }
    return localRows.get(itemId);
  };

  // Returns why this row cannot serve as the baseline, or null when it can.
  const notABaseline = async (row) => {
    if (typeof row.Title !== 'string' || row.Title === '') {
      return `row ${row.Id} in the list found in the other web has no nonempty Title, and a `
        + 'blank baseline compares equal to a blank-titled row of this web sharing its '
        + 'list-local id, so it cannot tell a column that resolved from one that repointed';
    }
    const local = await localRowAt(row.Id);
    if (!local.readable) {
      return `row ${row.Id} of this web's target list did not read back (HTTP ${local.status}), `
        + 'so this run cannot show the baseline tells the two webs apart';
    }
    if (local.row !== undefined && local.row.Title === row.Title) {
      return `row ${row.Id} in the list found in the other web carries the same Title as row `
        + `${row.Id} of this web's target list, so an expansion equal to it cannot tell a `
        + 'column that resolved from one that quietly repointed';
    }
    return null;
  };

  for (const candidate of webRows) {
    const rejected = notAnotherWeb(candidate.Id);
    if (rejected !== null) {
      noWebWhy = rejected;
      continue;
    }
    const lists = await spGetAt(candidate.Url, 'web/lists?$select=Id,BaseTemplate,Hidden&$top=100');
    // Ten candidates per web at most: this is a search for one fixture rather
    // than a survey of somebody's site.
    const usable = ((lists.ok && lists.body && lists.body.value) || [])
      .filter((l) => l.BaseTemplate === 100 && l.Hidden !== true).slice(0, 10);
    for (const candidateList of usable) {
      // Title comes back because the resolution row compares an expansion with
      // it. It is held in memory and never printed.
      const firstRow = await spGetAt(candidate.Url,
        `web/lists(guid'${asGuid(candidateList.Id)}')/items?$select=Id,Title&$top=5`);
      const itemRows = (firstRow.ok && firstRow.body && firstRow.body.value) || [];
      // Only a row that can tell the two webs apart is a baseline, and the
      // first row that cannot says why, because it is the one a weaker test
      // would have taken.
      let chosen;
      let whyNot = null;
      for (const row of itemRows) {
        const unusable = await notABaseline(row);
        if (unusable === null) {
          chosen = row;
          break;
        }
        if (whyNot === null) whyNot = unusable;
      }
      // A list holding such a row is preferred, because resolving a lookup
      // needs something on the far side to resolve to. Any other readable
      // list is the fallback, since the acceptance rows can still be asked
      // against it.
      if (chosen !== undefined) {
        const local = await localRowAt(chosen.Id);
        otherWeb = candidate;
        otherList = candidateList;
        otherItemId = chosen.Id;
        otherItemTitle = chosen.Title;
        otherItemBaseline = local.row === undefined
          ? `this web's target list holds no row ${chosen.Id}, so a column that repointed at it `
            + 'expands no row at all'
          : `its Title differs from the Title on row ${chosen.Id} of this web's target list, `
            + 'which is the row a column that repointed would expand';
        break;
      }
      if (otherWeb === null) {
        otherWeb = candidate;
        otherList = candidateList;
        if (!firstRow.ok) {
          otherItemWhy = `rows in the other web's list did not read back: HTTP ${firstRow.status}`;
        } else if (whyNot !== null) {
          otherItemWhy = whyNot;
        } else {
          otherItemWhy = 'the list found in the other web holds no rows, so there is nothing '
            + 'to resolve to';
        }
      }
    }
    if (otherItemId !== null) break;
  }
  // Which pair of webs this run measured, reported and never required: the
  // rule it is parked against covers webs in unrelated site collections as
  // well as a child subweb, and a row quoted on its own would otherwise read
  // as a result about any two webs. Site ids, like every other value the arm
  // prints, are GUIDs and carry no site name.
  let measuredAt = '';
  if (otherWeb !== null) {
    const thisSite = await spGet('site?$select=Id');
    const otherSite = await spGetAt(otherWeb.Url, 'site?$select=Id');
    const sameCollection = !readFailed(thisSite) && !readFailed(otherSite)
      && asGuid(thisSite.body.Id) === asGuid(otherSite.body.Id);
    const where = readFailed(thisSite) || readFailed(otherSite)
      ? `which site collection it is in did not read back (HTTP ${thisSite.status}/${otherSite.status})`
      : (sameCollection
        ? `in THIS site collection ${asGuid(thisSite.body.Id)}`
        : `in ANOTHER site collection ${asGuid(otherSite.body.Id)}, not this one `
          + `(${asGuid(thisSite.body.Id)})`);
    measuredAt = ` Measured at other web ${asGuid(otherWeb.Id)}, ${where}.`;
  }

  if (otherWeb === null) {
    otherItemWhy = `${noWebWhy}. There is no row over there for this run to read either`;
    record('field.cross-web.control-other-web-has-a-list', OTHER_WEB_QUESTION, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           `${OTHER_WEB_URL ? 'the supplied web' : 'web/webs'} answered HTTP ${webs.status} with `
           + `${webRows.length} candidate web(s): ${noWebWhy}. `
           + `Paste this on a site that has one, or point OTHER_WEB_URL at a web that does. The `
           + `arm reads a web and never creates one.`);
  } else {
    record('field.cross-web.control-other-web-has-a-list', OTHER_WEB_QUESTION, 'PASS',
           `other web ${asGuid(otherWeb.Id)}, its list ${asGuid(otherList.Id)},`
           + `${OTHER_WEB_URL ? ' supplied through OTHER_WEB_URL' : ' found through web/webs'}.`
           + measuredAt);
  }
  if (otherItemId === null) {
    record('field.cross-web.control-other-web-list-has-an-item', OTHER_ITEM_QUESTION,
           'NOT ESTABLISHED', otherItemWhy);
  } else {
    record('field.cross-web.control-other-web-list-has-an-item', OTHER_ITEM_QUESTION, 'PASS',
           `row ${otherItemId} in list ${asGuid(otherList.Id)}: ${otherItemBaseline}. `
           + 'Neither Title is printed.');
  }

  const bareControlHeld = await runControl(
    'field.cross-web.control-same-web-lookup-created', SAME_WEB_QUESTION, 'XwebControl',
    `<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="Xweb Control" Name="XwebControl"`
    + ` List="{${target.id}}" ShowField="Title"/>`);

  // The WebId row asks about the web BOUNDARY, so the attribute has to be
  // ruled out first as the thing being refused. A local WebId that
  // createfieldasxml will not take makes a cross-web refusal a result about
  // the spelling, and the bare control above would still pass.
  let webIdControlHeld = false;
  if (thisWebId === null || thisWebId === undefined) {
    record('field.cross-web.control-same-web-webid-accepted', SAME_WEB_WEBID_QUESTION,
           'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           `this web's Id did not read back (HTTP ${thisWeb.status}), so the control had no `
           + 'local web to name');
  } else {
    webIdControlHeld = await runControl(
      'field.cross-web.control-same-web-webid-accepted', SAME_WEB_WEBID_QUESTION,
      'XwebWebIdControl',
      `<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="Xweb WebId Control" Name="XwebWebIdControl"`
      + ` List="{${target.id}}" WebId="{${thisWebId}}" ShowField="Title"/>`);
  }

  // Why the arm never asked, rather than a fixed line: a candidate rejected for
  // being THIS web is a different failure from a site with no second web.
  const NO_OTHER_WEB = `no other web was established to point at (${noWebWhy}), so the `
    + 'question was never asked';
  const byListVoid = otherWeb === null ? NO_OTHER_WEB
    : (bareControlHeld ? null
      : 'the same-web control did not hold, so a refusal here would say the method did not '
        + 'work on this site rather than anything about the web boundary');
  const byWebIdVoid = otherWeb === null ? NO_OTHER_WEB
    : (webIdControlHeld ? null
      : 'the same-web WebId control did not hold, so a refusal here would be about the '
        + 'attribute rather than about the web boundary');

  let byList = null;
  if (byListVoid) {
    voidAll(['field.cross-web.createfieldasxml-other-web-refused'], byListVoid);
  } else {
    byList = await attemptLookup('XwebList',
      `<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="Xweb List" Name="XwebList"`
      + ` List="{${otherList.Id}}" ShowField="Title"/>`);
    record('field.cross-web.createfieldasxml-other-web-refused', BY_LIST_QUESTION,
           byList.asked ? (byList.ok ? 'ACCEPTED' : 'REFUSED') : 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           byList.detail + measuredAt);
  }

  let byWebId = null;
  if (byWebIdVoid) {
    voidAll(['field.cross-web.webid-attribute-refused'], byWebIdVoid);
  } else {
    byWebId = await attemptLookup('XwebListAndWeb',
      `<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="Xweb List And Web" Name="XwebListAndWeb"`
      + ` List="{${otherList.Id}}" WebId="{${otherWeb.Id}}" ShowField="Title"/>`);
    record('field.cross-web.webid-attribute-refused', BY_WEBID_QUESTION,
           byWebId.asked ? (byWebId.ok ? 'ACCEPTED' : 'REFUSED') : 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           byWebId.detail + measuredAt);
  }

  // A lookup id is list-local, so a column that quietly repointed at this web
  // stores the same number and expands a row of THIS web. Only the comparison
  // with the remote row's own Title separates that from a column that works.
  const resolveThrough = async (name) => {
    digest = await getDigest();
    const set = await spPost(sourceItems,
      { Title: `xweb-${name}`, [`${name}Id`]: otherItemId }, digest);
    if (!set.ok) {
      return { answered: true, resolved: false,
               detail: `setting it to row ${otherItemId} answered HTTP ${set.status}: `
                 + set.text.slice(0, 240) };
    }
    const itemId = set.body.Id;
    const stored = await spGet(`${sourceItems}(${itemId})?$select=${name}Id`);
    if (readFailed(stored)) {
      return { answered: false,
               detail: `the stored id did not read back (HTTP ${stored.status})` };
    }
    const storedId = stored.body[`${name}Id`];
    if (storedId !== otherItemId) {
      return { answered: true, resolved: false,
               detail: `stored id reads back ${JSON.stringify(storedId)}, not ${otherItemId}` };
    }
    const expanded = await spGet(`${sourceItems}(${itemId})?$select=${name}/Title&$expand=${name}`);
    if (readFailed(expanded)) {
      return { answered: false,
               detail: `stored id ${otherItemId}, but $expand answered HTTP ${expanded.status}, `
                 + 'which cannot tell a value that does not resolve from one this spelling '
                 + 'cannot read' };
    }
    const seenTitle = expanded.body[name] ? expanded.body[name].Title : undefined;
    const same = typeof seenTitle === 'string' && seenTitle === otherItemTitle;
    return { answered: true, resolved: same,
             detail: `stored id ${otherItemId}; the expansion carried ${titleKind(seenTitle)}, `
               + `${same ? 'the same as' : 'not'} the Title on that row` };
  };

  // One row per spelling rather than one verdict over both. The two are
  // separate requests that may answer differently, and a combined row has to
  // choose when they do: a column observed repointing at this web disproves
  // the claim whichever way the other column read, and reporting that as
  // unestablished would discard the observation that settled it.
  const SPELLINGS = [
    {
      name: 'XwebList', attempt: byList, unasked: byListVoid,
      readbackId: 'field.cross-web.list-spelling-targets-other-web',
      readbackQuestion: LIST_READBACK_QUESTION,
      resolveId: 'field.cross-web.list-spelling-resolves-remote-item',
      resolveQuestion: LIST_RESOLVE_QUESTION,
    },
    {
      name: 'XwebListAndWeb', attempt: byWebId, unasked: byWebIdVoid,
      readbackId: 'field.cross-web.webid-spelling-targets-other-web',
      readbackQuestion: WEBID_READBACK_QUESTION,
      resolveId: 'field.cross-web.webid-spelling-resolves-remote-item',
      resolveQuestion: WEBID_RESOLVE_QUESTION,
    },
  ];
  // A baseline that cannot discriminate is not a baseline, and the search
  // above leaves otherItemId null unless it found one that can.
  const noBaseline = otherItemId === null || typeof otherItemTitle !== 'string'
    || otherItemTitle === '';

  for (const spelling of SPELLINGS) {
    const spellingRows = [spelling.readbackId, spelling.resolveId];
    if (spelling.unasked) {
      voidAll(spellingRows, spelling.unasked);
      continue;
    }
    if (!spelling.attempt.asked || !spelling.attempt.ok) {
      record(spelling.readbackId, spelling.readbackQuestion, 'NOT APPLICABLE',
             `this run created no ${spelling.name} column, so there is nothing to read back: `
             + spelling.attempt.detail);
      record(spelling.resolveId, spelling.resolveQuestion, 'NOT APPLICABLE',
             `this run created no ${spelling.name} column, so there is nothing to set on a row: `
             + spelling.attempt.detail);
      continue;
    }
    const got = await spGet(`${sourceFields}/getbyinternalnameortitle('${spelling.name}')`
      + `?$select=LookupList,LookupWebId,LookupField,TypeAsString`);
    // A column that did not read back is not enrolled in the value
    // experiment: setting a lookup property no column carries is refused for
    // the name, which is not the web boundary answering.
    if (readFailed(got)) {
      voidAll(spellingRows,
              `${spelling.attempt.detail}, but the ${spelling.name} column did not read back `
              + `(HTTP ${got.status}), so this run has no field to read or to set a value on.`
              + measuredAt);
      continue;
    }
    const pointsOut = asGuid(got.body.LookupList) === asGuid(otherList.Id)
      && asGuid(got.body.LookupWebId) === asGuid(otherWeb.Id);
    record(spelling.readbackId, spelling.readbackQuestion, pointsOut ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL',
           `expected LookupList=${asGuid(otherList.Id)} LookupWebId=${asGuid(otherWeb.Id)}; `
           + `${spelling.name} reads back LookupList=${asGuid(got.body.LookupList)} `
           + `LookupWebId=${asGuid(got.body.LookupWebId)} `
           + `LookupField=${got.body.LookupField} TypeAsString=${got.body.TypeAsString}.`
           + measuredAt);

    // Resolution is asked whatever the metadata above read back: that
    // readback is an observation of this arm, and gating the stronger test on
    // it would hide a column that carries the row while reporting a web id
    // nobody expected.
    if (noBaseline) {
      record(spelling.resolveId, spelling.resolveQuestion, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             otherItemWhy, 'void');
      continue;
    }
    const seen = await resolveThrough(spelling.name);
    record(spelling.resolveId, spelling.resolveQuestion,
           seen.answered ? (seen.resolved ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL') : 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           `row ${otherItemId} in list ${asGuid(otherList.Id)}; `
           + `${spelling.name}: ${seen.detail}.` + measuredAt,
           seen.answered ? undefined : 'void');
  }

  record('BOOT', 'target + source lists, Status choice, LiveTitle calc and two target rows exist',
         'PASS', `target=${target.id} source=${source.id} open=${openId} closed=${closedId}`);

  report();
})();
